# Configuration

`{{project_slug}} config show` prints the effective configuration. Each layer
overrides the one before it: defaults, the config file, environment variables,
then flags. Flags only override when they are given.

The config file is TOML at `$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/{{project_slug}}/config.toml`, or
`~/.config/{{project_slug}}/config.toml` when `XDG_CONFIG_HOME` is unset. A
missing default file is fine. `--config PATH` reads that file instead and fails
when it doesn't exist. Unknown keys, malformed TOML and invalid values fail
with a `CONFIG` error that names the file.

| Key | Default | Environment | Flag |
|---|---|---|---|
| `greeting` | `"Hello, world!"` | `{{env_prefix}}_GREETING` | none |
| `output` | `"text"` | `{{env_prefix}}_OUTPUT` | `--json` / `--no-json` |
| `verbose` | `false` | `{{env_prefix}}_VERBOSE` | `--verbose` |
| `color` | `true` | `{{env_prefix}}_COLOR` | `--no-color` |

`greeting` must contain non-whitespace text. `output` is `text` or `json`.
Booleans accept `true`, `false`, `1` and `0`.

Global options go before the command: `{{project_slug}} --json config show`.
`--version` and errors that happen before settings load (an unknown command, a
bad flag) follow the explicit `--json` flag only, not the file or environment.
`--help` is always text.

## Output

Results go to stdout. Logs, errors and suggestions go to stderr. With
`--json`, stdout holds one JSON document and an error is one JSON document on
stderr: `{"error": {"code", "message", "suggestion"}}`. Logs are warnings and
above by default, debug with `--verbose`, and JSON records in JSON mode.
`NO_COLOR` (even empty) disables color, and color needs a terminal.

## Exit codes

| Exit | Codes | When |
|---|---|---|
| 0 | | Success, `--help`, `--version` |
| 1 | `INTERNAL` | An unexpected error |
| 2 | `INVALID_INPUT` | Unknown command or option, a missing or extra argument |
| 3 | `CONFIG` | A config file that can't be read or parsed, an unknown key, a bad value |
| 4 | `IO` | A failed read or write of a file the command works on |
| 130 | `INTERRUPTED` | Ctrl+C (Typer exits quietly), or input closed at a prompt |

An unexpected error prints `INTERNAL: unexpected error` without details;
`--verbose` logs the traceback to stderr.
